patches.behavior:
Stateless: reads the host's device snapshot every frame.

Each frame:
```
const d = ctx.services.device();
const preset = DEVICE_PRESETS.find((p) => p.id === d.preset); // @sonobe/core devices.ts
const [w, h] = finite2(d.screenSize);
ctx.output("screenSize", [w, h]);
ctx.output("safeArea", finite4(d.safeArea));          // [top, right, bottom, left]
ctx.output("screenScale", d.screenScale > 0 && Number.isFinite(d.screenScale) ? d.screenScale : 1);
ctx.output("orientation", orientationAngle(d));
ctx.output("landscape", w > h);
ctx.output("usesMouse", preset?.kind === "computer");
ctx.output("darkMode", d.darkMode === true);
ctx.output("deviceName", preset?.name ?? d.preset);
```

- Source of truth: the host fills `DeviceInfo` before frame 0 from the project's `device` settings (preset, size override, orientation) and keeps it current. Screen Size and Safe Area describe the **interface as drawn**: while the interface is in landscape, width and height are swapped and the insets come from the renderer's `orientSafeArea` (`packages/renderer/src/deviceFrame.ts`). The patch never re-derives them. The web player on a real phone reports the viewport size in points, `devicePixelRatio` as the scale, the `env(safe-area-inset-*)` insets, and `prefers-color-scheme`.
- Orientation: the physical rotation of the device in degrees, measured counterclockwise: 0 upright, 90 turned counterclockwise (top edge on the left), 180 upside down, 270 turned clockwise. It follows the device even while Interface Orientation keeps the interface from turning. The contract only carries `"portrait" | "landscape"`, so until `DeviceInfo.orientationAngle` exists, `orientationAngle` maps portrait → 0 and landscape → 90. Web hosts read `screen.orientation.angle`.
- Landscape: `width > height` of Screen Size, so it describes the interface, and a square screen reads false.
- Uses a Mouse: describes the simulated device, not the machine running the viewer. A phone preset in the desktop app reads false; the Desktop presets read true. Unknown preset ids read false.
- Dark Mode: the viewer's appearance setting in the editor, `prefers-color-scheme: dark` in the web player.
- Updates: outputs change on the first frame after the snapshot changes (device picker, viewer rotation, a resized custom device, an `orientation` InputEvent). The host snapshot is taken at step 1 of the frame pipeline, so every patch in a frame sees the same values.
- Frame 0: reports the starting device. There are no startup zeros.
- Loops: no inputs, so every output is a single value.
- Edge cases: an unknown preset id reports the raw id as Device Name. A non-positive or non-finite scale reports 1. Non-finite sizes or insets report 0 and warn once per restart.
- Platform and simulation: the deterministic runtime merges `RuntimeOptions.device` over the project's preset (Dark Mode false unless set). `orientation` InputEvents flip the orientation and swap Screen Size and Safe Area from the next frame.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: Device maps to `deviceName` and Uses a Mouse to `usesMouse`. Dark Mode (v72) and Safe Area (v114) are undocumented in Origami; Sonobe types Safe Area as point4d [top, right, bottom, left], matching the layout Padding property. Origami doesn't document its orientation angles, so imported comparisons against specific angles may need review.

State per loop index: `{ tilt: [0, 0, 0], acceleration: [0, 0, 0], rotationRate: [0, 0, 0], available: false }` (the last sample output while enabled).

Each frame:
```
if (ctx.input("enabled")) {
  const s = ctx.services.platform.deviceMotion?.();
  if (s) {
    state.available = true;
    state.acceleration = finite3(s.acceleration);
    state.rotationRate = finite3(s.rotationRate);
    state.tilt = s.attitude ? finite3(s.attitude) : tiltFromGravity(state.acceleration, state.tilt);
  }
  ctx.requestNextFrame();
} else {
  state.available = false;
}
ctx.output("tilt", state.tilt);
ctx.output("acceleration", state.acceleration);
ctx.output("rotationRate", state.rotationRate);
ctx.output("available", state.available);
```

- Units and axes: the host normalizes samples and the patch never converts. Axes are the device's own: x toward the right edge, y toward the top edge, z out of the screen.
  - Acceleration is in g including gravity, signed the way gravity pulls: upright portrait ≈ [0, −1, 0], face up on a table ≈ [0, 0, −1], right edge tipped down → x toward +1. Web hosts divide `accelerationIncludingGravity` by −9.80665 and correct for browsers that report the opposite sign, so a face-up device reads z ≈ −1 everywhere.
  - Rotation Rate is degrees per second: `DeviceMotionEvent.rotationRate` `[beta, gamma, alpha]` → [x, y, z].
  - Tilt comes from `DeviceOrientationEvent` `[beta, gamma, alpha]` in degrees: x front to back (−180..180), y side to side (−90..90), z heading (0..360, or 0 when unreported). `PlatformServices.deviceMotion().attitude` carries it in that order.
- `tiltFromGravity(g, previous)`, used when a sample has no attitude (including simulation): let `n = |g|`; if `n < 0.1` (free fall or no data) return `previous`; otherwise `x = atan2(−g[1], −g[2])` and `y = asin(clamp(g[0] / n, −1, 1))`, both in degrees, and `z = 0`. Face up → [0, 0, 0]; upright → [90, 0, 0]; right edge down → y = 90.
- Sampling: sensor events arrive between frames on their own schedule. The host keeps only the latest sample, read at step 1 of the frame pipeline, so values don't depend on frame rate.
- Permission: iOS's `DeviceMotionEvent.requestPermission()` must run inside a user gesture, which `evaluate` never is. When a document contains a deviceMotion patch and the platform needs permission, the web player shows a one-time Allow Motion button. Until permission is granted, `deviceMotion()` returns undefined and Available stays false. A denial doesn't re-prompt during the session.
- Available: false on frame 0 until a sample exists, on devices without sensors, on computers, and while disabled.
- Frame 0: vectors [0, 0, 0] and Available false unless the host already holds a sample.
- Loops: a looped Enabled gives one state per index; every enabled index reads the same sample.
- Edge cases: non-finite components output 0 and warn once per restart.
- Disabled: Available is false and the vectors hold their last values. The host may release its sensor listeners when no enabled deviceMotion patch remains.
- Platform and simulation: simulation reads `deviceMotion` InputEvents (acceleration and rotation rate, no attitude, so Tilt comes from `tiltFromGravity`). The latest event stays in effect until the next one. Before the first event, Available is false.
- Restart and dispose: state resets to zeros and false. Nothing to release; the host owns the listeners.
- Coming from Origami: Has Acceleration and Has Rotation Rate both import as `available`. Origami's rotation rate units are undocumented (probably radians per second, from Core Motion), so imported multipliers may need a ×57.3 review. The v113 headphone motion source has no web equivalent and imports as the device's own sensors.

State per loop index: `{ seconds: 0, milliseconds: 0, timeOfDay: 0 }` (the last values output while enabled).

Each frame:
```
if (ctx.input("enabled")) {
  const now = Math.floor(ctx.services.now());       // epoch ms
  if (Number.isFinite(now)) {
    state.seconds = Math.floor(now / 1000);
    state.milliseconds = now - state.seconds * 1000; // integer 0..999, also before 1970
    const d = new Date(now);
    state.timeOfDay = deterministic
      ? d.getUTCHours() * 3600 + d.getUTCMinutes() * 60 + d.getUTCSeconds() + d.getUTCMilliseconds() / 1000
      : d.getHours() * 3600 + d.getMinutes() * 60 + d.getSeconds() + d.getMilliseconds() / 1000;
  }
  ctx.requestNextFrame();
}
ctx.output("seconds", state.seconds);
ctx.output("milliseconds", state.milliseconds);
ctx.output("timeOfDay", state.timeOfDay);
```

- Clock source: `ctx.services.now()`, the wall clock of the device running the prototype. Never `ctx.time` (that restarts at 0) and never `Date.now`. Building a `Date` from the given milliseconds is fine; it only decomposes that moment.
- Time of Day: wall-clock seconds since local midnight, so on a daylight-saving change it follows the clock (the day has 23 or 25 hours, and the value jumps). `deterministic` means the runtime was created with `deterministic: true`; simulation uses UTC so traces match on every machine. The live web player uses the phone's own time zone.
- Clock jumps: if the system clock changes (manual change, network sync), the outputs follow, and Seconds can go backward.
- Frame 0: the current time. There's no zero startup frame.
- Disabled: the outputs hold their last values (0 when disabled from the start). Re-enabling jumps them to the current time.
- Loops: a looped Enabled gives one held set of values per index; every enabled index reads the same clock.
- Edge cases: a non-finite `now()` keeps the held values and warns once per restart.
- Platform and simulation: the deterministic runtime's `now()` starts at a fixed epoch and advances by the fixed dt each frame.
- Restart and dispose: nothing resets, because the device clock keeps running. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Seconds keeps its name. v88's millisecond output is undocumented; Sonobe defines Milliseconds as the 0–999 part of the current second, so Seconds × 1000 + Milliseconds is the full timestamp. v172's Enable maps to `enabled`.

Stateless.

Each frame:
```
const vibrate = ctx.services.platform.vibrate;
ctx.output("available", typeof vibrate === "function");
if (ctx.pulsed("vibrate")) {
  const seconds = Number.isFinite(ctx.input("duration")) ? ctx.input("duration") : 0;
  const ms = Math.round(Math.min(Math.max(seconds, 0), 10) * 1000);
  if (ms > 0) {
    if (vibrate) vibrate(ms);
    else ctx.services.log("log", "Vibrate: " + ms + " ms (this device can't vibrate)");
  }
}
```

- Pulses: one input, `vibrate`. It fires on an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean, so a held true buzzes once. Pulses on consecutive frames each restart the buzz.
- Duration: clamped to 0–10 s. Zero or negative durations do nothing and log nothing. Changing Duration while a buzz runs doesn't change that buzz.
- Overlap: the host's `vibrate(ms)` replaces any running vibration, as `navigator.vibrate` does, so a new pulse restarts the buzz with the new duration.
- User activation: Chromium ignores `navigator.vibrate` until the person has tapped or clicked the page, so a pulse at launch silently does nothing. Available still reads true.
- Frame 0: a pulse on frame 0 buzzes (subject to user activation).
- Loops: evaluated per index. Several indices pulsing in one frame each call `vibrate`; the host keeps the last call, which is the highest index.
- Side effects: at most one `vibrate` call per frame per index, and only on a pulse.
- Platform and simulation: hosts without a vibration API leave `platform.vibrate` undefined, so the desktop app and the deterministic runtime log instead and never touch hardware.
- Restart and dispose: `dispose(state, services)` calls `services.platform.vibrate?.(0)`, which stops any running buzz.
- Coming from Origami: the unlabeled input maps to `vibrate`. Origami's iPhone viewer plays a fixed system buzz of about 0.4 s, which the default Duration matches.

State per loop index: `{ id: 0, speaking: false, ended: null, endAt: 0 }`, where `ended` is `null`, `"ended"`, or `"interrupted"`.

Each frame:
```
const platform = ctx.services.platform;
if (ctx.pulsed("stop")) {
  if (state.speaking) {
    state.id++;                                   // ignore the stopped utterance's result
    state.speaking = false;
    platform.stopSpeaking?.();                    // web: speechSynthesis.cancel()
  }
} else if (ctx.pulsed("speak")) {
  const text = String(ctx.input("text")).trim();
  if (text !== "") {
    const id = ++state.id;
    const rate = clamp(finite(ctx.input("rate"), 1), 0.1, 10);
    const opts = { rate, pitch: clamp(finite(ctx.input("pitch"), 1), 0, 2), volume: clamp(finite(ctx.input("volume"), 1), 0, 1), voice: ctx.input("voice") || undefined };
    state.speaking = true;
    state.ended = false;
    const result = platform.speak ? platform.speak(text, opts) : ctx.services.log("log", "Text to Speech: " + JSON.stringify(text));
    if (isThenable(result)) {
      state.endAt = Infinity;
      result.then((how) => { if (state.id === id) state.ended = how !== "interrupted" ? "ended" : "interrupted"; }, () => { if (state.id === id) state.ended = "interrupted"; });
    } else {
      state.endAt = ctx.time + Math.max(0.5, text.length / (15 * rate)); // estimate: 15 characters per second at rate 1
    }
  }
}
if (state.speaking && (state.ended || ctx.time >= state.endAt)) {
  if (state.ended !== "interrupted") ctx.pulse("finished");
  state.speaking = false;
  state.ended = false;
}
if (state.speaking) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("speaking", state.speaking);
```

- Pulses: `speak` and `stop`. If both arrive in one frame, Stop wins and Speak is ignored. Pulses on consecutive frames each count: a second Speak restarts with the current Text.
- Interrupting: the web has one speech queue per page, so a host `speak` cancels anything the prototype is saying (other patches and loop indices included) before starting. An interrupted or stopped utterance never pulses Finished.
- Async: a host that can report completion returns a promise from `speak` that resolves with `"ended"` or `"interrupted"` (web: the utterance's `end` and `error` events). The callback only stores the result, the next `evaluate` applies it, and results from replaced utterances are ignored by `id`. A host that returns nothing gets the length-based estimate instead.
- Voice (host): empty → the system default. Otherwise the first voice whose name contains the text (case-insensitive), then the first whose language starts with it (`en-GB`). No match → the default voice and one warning per restart. Voices load asynchronously; the host waits up to 1 s for `voiceschanged` before falling back.
- Retargeting: changing Text, Voice, Rate, Pitch, or Volume while speaking affects only the next Speak.
- Frame 0: Speaking is false. A Speak pulse on frame 0 starts speaking, but iOS Safari stays silent until the person has tapped the page.
- Loops: per-index state. When several indices pulse Speak in one frame, they're evaluated in index order, so the highest index is what you hear; the others end as interrupted.
- Edge cases: empty or whitespace-only Text does nothing (no Speaking, no Finished). Non-finite Rate, Pitch, or Volume use their defaults.
- Muted: never speaks; Speaking false and Finished never fires.
- Platform and simulation: the deterministic runtime, and any host running with audio muted (QA runs launched with `--mute-audio`), must leave `platform.speak` undefined. System speech engines on macOS and Windows play through the OS and bypass Chromium's audio mute. The patch then logs the text and uses the estimate, so traces of Speaking and Finished stay deterministic.
- Restart and dispose: `dispose` calls `platform.stopSpeaking?.()`; state resets.
- Coming from Origami: v212 added the patch without published ports. Importers match the labels Text, Speak (or Play), Stop, Voice, Rate, Pitch, and Volume. If Origami's Rate uses Apple's 0–1 scale (default 0.5), importers double it.

State per instance: a cache `{ patternRef, plan }` for the last parsed Pattern (not per index, since it's derived data).

Each frame:
```
const platform = ctx.services.platform;
const type = ctx.input("type");
const plan = vibrationPlan(type, ctx.input("pattern"));     // number[] | null, cached by Pattern identity
const native = platform.haptic;   // undefined where the host has none
ctx.output("available", native ? native.supports(type) : plan !== null && typeof platform.vibrate === "function");
if (ctx.pulsed("play")) {
  if (native?.supports(type)) native.play(type, type === "customPattern" ? ctx.input("pattern") : undefined);
  else if (plan && platform.vibrate) platform.vibrate(plan);
  else ctx.services.log("log", "Haptic: " + type + " (no haptics on this device)");
}
```

- `vibrationPlan` (milliseconds, alternating on and off): vibrate `[400]`; selection `[8]`; impactLight `[12]`; impactMedium `[20]`; impactHeavy `[35]`; notificationSuccess `[15, 80, 25]`; notificationWarning `[25, 100, 25]`; notificationError `[20, 60, 20, 60, 30]`; alignment and levelChange `null` (a trackpad click has no phone equivalent); customPattern → `ahapToVibration(pattern)`.
- `ahapToVibration(p)`: take `p.Pattern` entries with an `Event`. `HapticTransient` at `Time` t becomes the span [t, t + 0.012]; `HapticContinuous` becomes [t, t + max(0, EventDuration)]. Audio events and parameter curves are ignored, and intensity and sharpness can't be expressed as vibration. Sort the spans by start, merge overlaps, drop anything past 10 s, and emit `[on, off, on, …]` in whole milliseconds, prefixed with `[0, firstStart]` when the first event starts after 0. When Pattern isn't an object with a `Pattern` array or has no haptic events, the plan is null and one warning per restart names the problem.
- Pulses: one input, `play`. Consecutive-frame pulses each play. A new play replaces a running vibration pattern (`navigator.vibrate` semantics); native haptics overlap as the OS allows.
- Retargeting: changing Type or Pattern affects the next Play only.
- User activation: Chromium ignores vibration until the person has tapped the page, so a Play at launch may do nothing.
- Frame 0: a pulse on frame 0 plays.
- Loops: per index; several indices pulsing in one frame each play, and for vibration the highest index's pattern is the one felt.
- Edge cases: an unknown Type key imports as `impactLight` with a warning.
- Side effects: at most one play per frame per index, only on a pulse.
- Platform and simulation: hosts without haptics or vibration (desktop app, deterministic runtime) log instead and never touch hardware.
- Restart and dispose: `dispose(state, services)` calls `services.platform.vibrate?.(0)` to stop a running pattern.
- Coming from Origami: imports Haptic (the Haptic iOS component, `origami.haptic.ios` / `origami.HapticiOS`), Haptic Player (`builtin.hapticPlayer`), and Trackpad Haptic (`origami.haptic.macos`). Type options map by label with the platform suffix removed: "Selection (iOS)" → `selection`, "Alignment (macOS)" → `alignment`, "Custom Pattern (iOS)" → `customPattern`. The AHAP port and the component's File port map to `pattern`; importers load the .ahap file's JSON as the literal.

State per loop index: `{ latitude: 0, longitude: 0, name: "", accuracy: 0, available: false, loading: false, errorMessage: "", watch: null, fix: null, fixError: null }`.

Each frame:
```
const override = ctx.input("override");
if (!ctx.input("enabled")) {
  stopWatch(state);                                   // state.watch?.stop(); state.watch = null
  state.available = false;
  state.loading = false;
} else if (override !== "current") {
  stopWatch(state);
  const place = PLACES[override] ?? PLACES.sanFrancisco;
  Object.assign(state, { latitude: place.latitude, longitude: place.longitude, name: place.name, accuracy: 0, available: true, loading: false, errorMessage: "" });
} else {
  const geo = ctx.services.platform.geolocation;   // undefined where the host has none
  if (!geo) {
    Object.assign(state, { available: false, loading: false, errorMessage: "Location isn't available here. Choose an override city." });
  } else {
    if (!state.watch) {
      state.loading = !state.available;
      state.watch = geo.watch((fix) => { state.fix = fix; }, (message) => { state.fixError = message; });
    }
    if (state.fix) {
      const { latitude, longitude, accuracy } = state.fix;
      Object.assign(state, { latitude, longitude, accuracy, name: formatCoordinates(latitude, longitude), available: true, loading: false, errorMessage: "" });
      state.fix = null;
    }
    if (state.fixError) {
      Object.assign(state, { loading: false, errorMessage: state.fixError });
      state.fixError = null;
    }
    if (state.loading) ctx.requestNextFrame();
  }
}
ctx.output("latitude", state.latitude);
ctx.output("longitude", state.longitude);
ctx.output("name", state.name);
ctx.output("available", state.available);
ctx.output("accuracy", state.accuracy);
ctx.output("loading", state.loading);
ctx.output("errorMessage", state.errorMessage);
```

- Places (`PLACES`, decimal degrees of each city center): `sanFrancisco` San Francisco (37.7749, -122.4194); `newYork` New York (40.7128, -74.006); `mexicoCity` Mexico City (19.4326, -99.1332); `saoPaulo` São Paulo (-23.5505, -46.6333); `london` London (51.5074, -0.1278); `paris` Paris (48.8566, 2.3522); `lagos` Lagos (6.5244, 3.3792); `capeTown` Cape Town (-33.9249, 18.4241); `dubai` Dubai (25.2048, 55.2708); `mumbai` Mumbai (19.076, 72.8777); `singapore` Singapore (1.3521, 103.8198); `tokyo` Tokyo (35.6762, 139.6503); `sydney` Sydney (-33.8688, 151.2093).
- Host service (web): `watch` wraps `navigator.geolocation.watchPosition` with `enableHighAccuracy: false` and `maximumAge: 5000` and returns `{ stop() }` calling `clearWatch`. Error messages: permission denied → "Location permission was denied.", position unavailable → "The device couldn't find its location.", timeout → "Finding the location took too long.", insecure page → "Location needs a secure (https) page." A host shares one browser watch among all Location patches. Callbacks only store data, and the next `evaluate` applies it.
- `formatCoordinates(lat, lon)`: 4 decimals with hemisphere letters, e.g. `37.7749° N, 122.4194° W`.
- Permission: requested once, on the first frame a Location patch is enabled on Current Location in a live viewer. After a denial, Error Message stays set and there's no re-prompt this session.
- Updates: the watch keeps reporting as the device moves; the outputs hold the last fix between readings. An error after a fix keeps the last fix and Available, and sets Error Message.
- Retargeting: switching Override to a city stops the watch and outputs the city on that frame. Switching back to Current Location starts a new watch, and the outputs keep the city's values until the first fix arrives.
- Frame 0: an override city outputs immediately. Current Location starts with Loading true and zeros.
- Loops: per-index state (a looped Override lists several cities); indices on Current Location share the host's single watch.
- Edge cases: non-finite coordinates from the host are ignored. An unknown Override key imports as San Francisco with one warning.
- Disabled: Available and Loading false, the other outputs hold, and the watch stops.
- Async: see Host service; results from a stopped watch are ignored because `stopWatch` clears `state.watch` and the host drops its callbacks.
- Platform and simulation: the deterministic runtime has no geolocation service, so override cities work and Current Location reports Available false with "Location isn't available in simulation." No permission prompt ever runs headless.
- Restart and dispose: `dispose` stops the watch; state resets.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's override list isn't documented. Importers map a place whose name matches a Sonobe city and otherwise use Current Location with a warning. Origami's Name may show a place name for the real location; Sonobe shows coordinates.

Stateless: polls the host's controller snapshot every frame.

Each frame:
```
const slot = Math.max(0, Math.floor(finite(ctx.input("controller"), 0)));
const pad = ctx.services.platform.gamepads?.()[slot];   // undefined where the host has none
if (!pad?.connected) return outputIdle();                  // false, 0, [0, 0], [0, 0, 0]
const pressed = (i) => pad.buttons[i]?.pressed === true;
const amount = (i) => clamp(finite(pad.buttons[i]?.value, 0), 0, 1);
const dz = clamp(finite(ctx.input("deadZone"), 0.1), 0, 0.99);
ctx.output("connected", true);
ctx.output("buttonA", pressed(0));      ctx.output("buttonB", pressed(1));
ctx.output("buttonX", pressed(2));      ctx.output("buttonY", pressed(3));
ctx.output("leftShoulder", pressed(4)); ctx.output("rightShoulder", pressed(5));
ctx.output("leftTrigger", amount(6));   ctx.output("rightTrigger", amount(7));
ctx.output("dpad", [(pressed(15) ? 1 : 0) - (pressed(14) ? 1 : 0), (pressed(13) ? 1 : 0) - (pressed(12) ? 1 : 0)]);
ctx.output("leftThumbstick", deadZone([pad.axes[0], pad.axes[1]], dz));
ctx.output("rightThumbstick", deadZone([pad.axes[2], pad.axes[3]], dz));
ctx.output("home", pressed(16));
ctx.output("menu", pressed(9));         ctx.output("options", pressed(8));
ctx.output("leftThumbstickButton", pressed(10)); ctx.output("rightThumbstickButton", pressed(11));
ctx.output("acceleration", finite3(pad.motion?.acceleration));
ctx.output("rotationRate", finite3(pad.motion?.rotationRate));
```

- Layout: indices follow the W3C Gamepad "standard" mapping (0 bottom face, 1 right face, 2 left face, 3 top face, 4/5 shoulders, 6/7 triggers, 8 select/view, 9 start/menu, 10/11 stick presses, 12–15 D-pad up/down/left/right, 16 home). When `pad.mapping !== "standard"`, the host remaps known controllers; otherwise the raw indices are used and one warning per restart says the buttons may not match.
- Axes: stick y is positive downward, as the Gamepad API reports it, so up is −1 and down is +1, matching layer coordinates. The D-pad is discrete: each axis is −1, 0, or 1.
- `deadZone(v, dz)`: radial. With `n = |v|`, return [0, 0] when `n ≤ dz`; otherwise scale v by `min(1, (n − dz) / (1 − dz)) / n`. Non-finite axis values count as 0.
- Timing: the host snapshots `navigator.getGamepads()` at step 1 of each frame, so all outputs in a frame agree. Buttons and triggers are states, true or nonzero while held; a pulse input they're wired into fires once per press.
- Privacy gate: browsers expose no controller until a button is pressed after the page loads, so Connected stays false until then.
- Slots: Controller indexes `getGamepads()` slots, which don't shift when another controller disconnects.
- Motion: no browser reports controller motion today, so Acceleration and Rotation Rate stay [0, 0, 0] unless a native host fills `pad.motion` (same units as Device Motion: g and degrees per second).
- Frame 0: idle until the host's first snapshot shows a controller.
- Loops: a looped Controller reads one controller per index, for multiplayer prototypes.
- Edge cases: negative, fractional, or non-finite Controller values floor and clamp to 0 or more; a slot with no controller outputs idle values.
- Platform and simulation: the deterministic runtime has no controller events and outputs idle values.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release.
- Coming from Origami: A, B, X, Y, L1, R1, L2, R2, D-pad, Left Thumbstick, Right Thumbstick, Home, Acceleration, and Rotation map as listed in `origamiPorts`. Origami's y axis points up (top right is [1, 1]), so importers insert a Multiply by [1, −1] on each connected D-pad and thumbstick wire. Origami's L2 and R2 were booleans before v110; a trigger wired into a boolean port still reads as pressed above 0.

State per instance: `{ current: "portrait", lastDevice: null }`.

Each frame:
```
const d = ctx.services.device();
const device = deviceOrientation(d);   // "portrait" | "landscapeLeft" | "landscapeRight" | "upsideDown"
const allowed = (o) => ctx.input(o) === true && deviceSupports(d, o);
if (state.lastDevice === null) {
  const start = ctx.input("startIn");
  state.current = deviceSupports(d, start) ? start : "portrait";
} else if ((device !== state.lastDevice || ctx.changed(device)) && device !== state.current && allowed(device)) {
  state.current = device;
}
state.lastDevice = device;
ctx.output("orientation", state.current);
ctx.output("landscape", state.current === "landscapeLeft" || state.current === "landscapeRight");
```

- Rule: the interface turns only on an event, either the device rotating to an allowed direction or the input for the device's current direction turning on. Turning a direction off never turns the interface away from it, so switching off both landscape inputs while in landscape locks it there until the device turns to an allowed direction.
- `deviceOrientation(d)`: the device's physical or simulated rotation. With `DeviceInfo.orientationAngle`, 0 → portrait, 90 → landscapeLeft, 180 → upsideDown, 270 → landscapeRight. Hosts that don't report an angle give only `"portrait" | "landscape"`, and landscape maps to landscapeLeft.
- `deviceSupports(d, o)`: false for `upsideDown` on phone presets whose cutout is `island` or `notch`; true otherwise, including custom devices.
- Host integration: the viewer reads the `orientation` output of the controlling patch through `rt.getValue`. It rotates the drawn interface, and from the next frame the host swaps Device Info's Screen Size and Safe Area (`orientSafeArea`). At start and restart, the desktop viewer rotates its simulated device to Start In. On a real phone, the web player can't lock the browser's own rotation, so when the physical and interface orientations differ it counter-rotates the prototype by the difference. Proposed contract alternative: `RuntimeServices.setInterfaceOrientation(o)`.
- No patch: the interface keeps the project's `device.orientation` (portrait unless the project is landscape), and Device Info's Orientation still follows the device.
- Controlling patch: the first interfaceOrientation patch by id in the root component. Others (and any inside components) still compute outputs but don't drive the viewer, with one warning per restart.
- Pulses: none. Inputs are states.
- Frame 0: applies Start In. Portrait, Landscape Left, and the others only matter from frame 1.
- Loops: a looped input evaluates per index, but only index 0 drives the viewer.
- Edge cases: an unknown Start In key falls back to portrait with one warning.
- Muted: the viewer ignores a muted patch, so the interface keeps the project's orientation; Orientation outputs portrait and Landscape false.
- Platform and simulation: `orientation` InputEvents rotate the simulated device, and the rule above decides whether the interface follows. Nothing is released headless.
- Restart and dispose: `lastDevice` resets to null, so Start In applies again.
- Coming from Origami: ports keep their names and defaults (Portrait, Landscape Left, and Landscape Right on; Upside Down off; Start In Portrait). Origami's viewer adds this patch from its settings; in Sonobe you insert it like any patch.

State per instance: `{ from: 0, to: 0, elapsed: DURATION, progress: 0, measured: {} }`, where `DURATION = 0.35` s and `measured` holds the last real height per keyboard type.

Each frame:
```
const kb = ctx.services.platform.softKeyboard?.();     // { visible, height, keyboardType } | undefined
const d = ctx.services.device();
const choice = ctx.input("keyboardType");
const type = choice === "auto" ? (kb?.keyboardType ?? "default") : choice;
if (kb?.visible && Number.isFinite(kb.height) && kb.height > 0) state.measured[type] = kb.height;
const height = state.measured[type] ?? estimateHeight(d, type);
const target = kb?.visible ? 1 : 0;
if (target !== state.to) { state.from = state.progress; state.to = target; state.elapsed = 0; }
state.elapsed = Math.min(state.elapsed + ctx.dt, DURATION);
const t = state.elapsed / DURATION;
state.progress = state.from + (state.to - state.from) * (1 - (1 - t) ** 3);   // cubic ease out
if (t < 1) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("visibleHeight", height * state.progress);
ctx.output("height", height);
ctx.output("progress", state.progress);
ctx.output("visible", target === 1);
```

- `estimateHeight(d, type)` in points, including the home-indicator area: classify the preset by `kind` (a custom device is a phone when its shorter side is under 600 pt, otherwise a tablet). Phone portrait: 336, or 250 for number and phone. Phone landscape: 209, or 171 for number and phone. Tablet portrait: 313. Tablet landscape: 398. Computers and watches: 0.
- Host (desktop viewer): `softKeyboard()` reports visible while a Text Field layer is focused in a phone or tablet preset, with `height` from the table and `keyboardType` from the field's Keyboard property. The viewer may draw a placeholder keyboard over the bottom of the screen; the patch only reports metrics.
- Host (web player on a phone): visible while a text field is focused and either `navigator.virtualKeyboard.boundingRect.height > 0` or `innerHeight − visualViewport.height > 120` CSS pixels; `height` is that covered height converted to prototype points with the viewer scale. Browsers don't expose the keyboard's animation, so Progress always comes from the ease above.
- Retargeting: a keyboard that hides mid-slide eases from the current progress back to 0 over the full 0.35 s.
- Pulses: none.
- Frame 0: hidden, Progress 0, and Height from the estimate.
- Loops: a looped Keyboard Type gives per-index Height and Visible Height; the slide state is shared by the instance because there's one keyboard.
- Edge cases: a non-finite or non-positive host height falls back to the last measured height, then to the estimate.
- Muted: outputs 0 and false.
- Platform and simulation: the deterministic runtime has no keyboard service, so Visible stays false and Height reports the estimate.
- Restart and dispose: state resets, including measured heights. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Keyboard Info (`builtin.keyboard.info`). Type options map to Sonobe's: Default, ASCII Only, and Twitter → default; Numbers and Punctuation, Number Pad, Decimal Pad, and ASCII Numbers → number; Phone Pad and Name or Phone → phone; Email Address → email; URL and Web Search → url. Appearence [sic] maps to `appearance`. Height and Progress keep their names; Origami doesn't document whether its Height is 0 while hidden.

State per loop index: `{ gen: 0, link: null, connectError: null, dropped: false, incoming: null, ops: Promise.resolve(), opError: null, connected: false, loading: false, value: 0, text: "", deviceName: "", errorMessage: "", notifying: null }`.

Host service (`PlatformServices.bluetooth`):
```
interface BleService { available: boolean; connect(opts: { service: string; characteristic: string; namePrefix?: string }): Promise<BleLink>; }
interface BleLink {
  name: string; canRead: boolean; canNotify: boolean;
  read(): Promise<Uint8Array>; write(bytes: Uint8Array): Promise<void>; setNotifications(on: boolean): Promise<void>;
  onValue(cb: (bytes: Uint8Array) => void): void; onDisconnect(cb: () => void): void; disconnect(): void;
}
```

Each frame:
```
const ble = ctx.services.platform.bluetooth;
const fail = (message) => { state.errorMessage = message; state.error = true; };
// 1. Apply async results stored by callbacks (callbacks never call ctx.output).
if (state.link && !state.connected) { state.connected = true; state.loading = false; state.deviceName = state.link.name; state.error = false; state.errorMessage = ""; queue(state, () => state.link.canRead ? state.link.read().then(store) : null); }
if (state.connectError) { state.loading = false; fail(state.connectError); state.connectError = null; }
if (state.dropped) { resetLink(state); fail("The device disconnected."); state.dropped = false; }
if (state.opError) { fail(state.opError); state.opError = null; }
// 2. Commands. Disconnect beats Connect; Write runs before Read.
if (ctx.pulsed("disconnect")) { state.gen++; state.link?.disconnect(); resetLink(state); }
else if (ctx.pulsed("connect") && !state.connected && !state.loading) {
  if (!ble?.available) { fail("Bluetooth LE isn't available in this browser."); ctx.services.log("log", "Bluetooth LE: connect (no Bluetooth here)"); }
  else {
    const gen = ++state.gen;
    state.loading = true; state.error = false; state.errorMessage = "";
    ble.connect({ service: normalizeUuid(ctx.input("serviceUuid")), characteristic: normalizeUuid(ctx.input("characteristicUuid")), namePrefix: ctx.input("namePrefix") || undefined })
      .then((link) => {
        if (state.gen !== gen) return link.disconnect();
        link.onValue((bytes) => { if (state.gen === gen) state.incoming = bytes; });
        link.onDisconnect(() => { if (state.gen === gen) state.dropped = true; });
        state.link = link;
      }, (err) => { if (state.gen === gen) state.connectError = describeError(err); });
  }
}
if (ctx.pulsed("write")) state.connected ? encodeAndQueueWrite(state, ctx.input("writeValue"), ctx.input("format")) : fail("Connect before writing.");
if (ctx.pulsed("read")) state.connected ? queue(state, () => state.link.read().then(store)) : fail("Connect before reading.");
const wantNotify = state.connected && ctx.input("notifications") && state.link.canNotify;
if (state.connected && state.notifying !== wantNotify) { state.notifying = wantNotify; queue(state, () => state.link.setNotifications(wantNotify)); }
// 3. Deliver the newest value.
if (state.incoming) { [state.value, state.text] = decode(state.incoming, ctx.input("format")); state.incoming = null; ctx.pulse("received"); }
if (state.loading || state.connected) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("value", state.value); ctx.output("text", state.text);
ctx.output("connected", state.connected); ctx.output("deviceName", state.deviceName);
ctx.output("errorMessage", state.errorMessage); ctx.output("loading", state.loading);
ctx.output("available", ble?.available === true); ctx.output("error", state.error === true);
```

- `store(bytes)` sets `state.incoming = bytes`. `queue(state, op)` chains `op` onto `state.ops`, so GATT operations run one at a time (Web Bluetooth rejects overlapping ones); a rejection stores `describeError(err)` in `state.opError`, and results for an older `gen` are dropped. `resetLink` clears `link`, `connected`, `loading`, `notifying`, and `deviceName` but keeps the last Value and Text.
- `normalizeUuid(s)`: trim and lowercase. 4 hex digits (optionally `0x`-prefixed) → `0000xxxx-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb`; 8 hex digits → `xxxxxxxx-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb`; a 36-character UUID passes through; anything else is treated as a GATT name with spaces turned into underscores (`heart rate` → `heart_rate`). The host reports unknown names as errors.
- `decode(bytes, format)`: little-endian `DataView` reads for the numeric formats; too few bytes → Value 0 and one warning per restart. Text for numeric formats is the bytes as uppercase hex pairs (`0A FF`). `text` → UTF-8 decode, and Value is `Number(text)` when finite, else 0. `hex` → Text is the hex pairs and Value is the first byte.
- Write encoding: numeric formats parse Write Value as a number, round integers, and clamp to the format's range. Non-numbers fail with "Write Value isn't a number." `text` → UTF-8 bytes. `hex` → pairs with spaces, colons, and `0x` ignored; odd or invalid digits fail. More than 512 bytes fails.
- Host (web): `connect` calls `navigator.bluetooth.requestDevice({ filters: [{ services: [service], namePrefix }] })`, then `gatt.connect()`, `getPrimaryService`, and `getCharacteristic`. Error text: user cancelled → "No device was chosen."; missing user activation → "Connect needs a tap first."; unsupported service or characteristic → "The device doesn't offer that service or characteristic.". The desktop app answers Electron's `select-bluetooth-device` event with its own chooser.
- Pulses: `connect`, `disconnect`, `read`, `write`. Disconnect beats Connect in one frame. Connect while connected or connecting does nothing. Write is queued before Read in the same frame, so Read sees the written value. Consecutive-frame pulses each queue an operation.
- Retargeting: changing Service UUID, Characteristic UUID, or Name Prefix while connected takes effect on the next Connect (browsers only grant services named in the chooser), with one warning. Changing Notifications while connected turns them on or off.
- Values: when several values arrive between frames, the newest wins and Received pulses once.
- Frame 0: disconnected; Available reflects the platform.
- Loops: per-index state; each index keeps its own connection and chooser.
- Edge cases: empty UUIDs fail on Connect with "Service UUID is empty." or "Characteristic UUID is empty."
- Muted: never connects, reads, or writes; outputs zeros.
- Async: see the pseudo-code; nothing calls `ctx.output` from a callback, and `gen` discards results from replaced connections.
- Platform and simulation: the deterministic runtime has no Bluetooth service, so Available is false and Connect logs instead of scanning.
- Restart and dispose: `dispose` disconnects the link and drops its callbacks. After a restart the patch starts disconnected; reconnecting needs another tap.
- Coming from Origami: v217 added a family of Bluetooth LE patches without published names or ports. Importers map matching labels (Connect, Disconnect, Service UUID, Characteristic UUID, Read, Write, Value, Connected) onto this patch and warn about anything else.
